ur. Let me calculate that. 15 × 16. Hmm, 10 × 16 is 160, and 5 × 16 is 80, so adding them gives 240. So the second job makes $240.\n\nNow, add those two amounts together: $600 + $240. Let me add them. 600 + 200 is 800, plus 40 more is 840. So total earnings per week should be $840.\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. \n\nFirst job: 30 hours at $20/hour. 30 × 20. Yes, 30 × 20 is definitely 600.\n\nSecond job: half of 30 hours is 15 hours. 15 hours at 80% of $20 per hour. Which is 15 × 16. Let me calculate again. 15 × 10 is 150, 15 × 6 is 90, 150 + 90 is 240. So that's correct. \n\nAdding them: 600 + 240. 600 + 200 is 800, so +40 makes it 840. Yep, that seems right.\n\nIs there any chance I messed up percentages? Wait, he was 20% less at the second job. So if his first job is 20%, does that mean the second is 20% less than 20%? Or is it 20% less than the original rate? Which is $20, so 80% of 20, which is 16. So that's correct.\n\nSo all steps check out. Therefore, James earns $840 per week. The answer should be in a box, so \\boxed{840}.\n</think>\n\nJames earns $20 per hour at his main job and 20% less per hour at his second job. \n\n- At his first job:\n  - He works 30 hours at $20/hour.\n  - Earnings: $30 × $20 = **$600**\n\n- At his second job:\n  - He works half the amount at his first job, which is $15 hours.\n  - His hourly rate is 80% of $20, which is $16.\n  - Earnings: $15 × $16 = **$240**\n\n- Total weekly earnings: $600 + $240 = **$840**\n\n$$\n\\boxed{840}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "James earns $20 an hour while working at his main job.  He earns 20% less while working his second job.  He works 30 hours at his main job and half that much at his second job.  How much does he earn per week?", "answer": "James earns 20*.2=$<<20*.2=4>>4 less while working his second job\nSo he earns 20-4=$<<20-4=16>>16 an hour\nAt his first job he earns 20*30=$<<20*30=600>>600\nHe works 30/2=<<30/2=15>>15 