ait, 4 inches times 15 gallons per inch is 60 gallons. Yes, right. So 4 inches = 60 gallons. Got that.\n\nThen on Tuesday, it rained 3 inches. So that would be 3 * 15 = 45 gallons. Let me check: 3 inches times 15 gallons per inch is 45 gallons. Yes.\n\nNow, total gallons collected would be 60 gallons from Monday plus 45 gallons from Tuesday. Let me add those up. 60 + 45. Hmm, 60 + 40 is 100, then +5 more is 105 gallons. So total of 105 gallons.\n\nNow, the price is $1.2 per gallon. To find the total money, I need to multiply the total gallons by 1.2 dollars per gallon. So 105 * 1.2. Let me compute that. 100 * 1.2 is 120, and 5 * 1.2 is 6. So adding those together, 120 + 6 = 126 dollars. Therefore, James made $126 from selling all the water.\n\nWait, let me check again if I did that correctly. First, Monday: 4 inches * 15 gallons = 60 gallons. Correct. Tuesday: 3 inches * 15 = 45 gallons. Correct. Total gallons 60+45=105. Then 105*1.2. Yes, 105 times 1.2. Alternatively, 105 * 1.2 can also be calculated as 105*(1 + 0.2) = 105 + 21 = 126. Yep, that adds up. Seems correct.\n\nI don't think I made any calculation mistakes here. So the answer should be $126. Let me check once more if there's any alternative way that might have gone wrong. For example, maybe confusing inches with gallons. But no, the problem says he collects 15 gallons per inch, so each inch gives 15 gallons. So the math seems straightforward. Yeah, I think that is correct.\n</think>\n\nJames collects rainwater as follows:\n\n- **Monday**: 4 inches, which collects 4 inches * 15 gallons/inch = $60$ gallons.\n- **Tuesday**: 3 inches, which collects 3 inches * 15 gallons/inch = $45$ gallons.\n\nThe total collection of water is therefore:\n$$\n60 \\text{ gallons} + 45 \\text{ gallons} = 105 \\text{ gallons}\n$$\n\nJames sells the water for $1.2 per gallon, so the total money earned is:\n$$\n105 \\text{ gallons} \\times 1.2 \\text{ dollars/gallon} = 126 \\text{ dollars}\n$$\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer:**\n$$\n\\boxed{126}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "James has a rainwater collection barrel.  For each inch of rain he collects 15 gallons.  On Monday it rained 4 inches and on Tuesday it rained 3 inches.  He can sell water for $1.2 per gallon.  How much money did he make from selling all the water?", "answer": "It rained 3+4=<<3+4=7>>7 inches\nSo he collected 7*15=<<7*15=105>>105 gallons\nSo he makes 105*1.2=$<<105*1.2=126>>126 from selling the water\n#### 126", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: James has a rainwater collection barrel.  For each inch of rain he collects 15 gallons.  On Monday it rained 4 inches and on Tuesday it rained 3 inches.  He can sell water for $1.2 per gallon.  How much money did he make from selling all the water?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["126"], "solution": "126"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 7801, 702, 264, 11174, 12987, 4426, 20311, 13, 220, 1752, 1817, 17122, 315, 11174, 566, 51207, 220, 16, 20, 50122, 13, 220, 1913, 7014, 432, 435, 2627, 220, 19, 14924, 323, 389, 7589, 432, 435, 2627, 220], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1753, 3220, 7801, 1865, 504, 11236, 279, 3015, 13, 88190, 11, 1156, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 3535, 279, 3491, 3019, 553, 3019, 382, 4416, 11, 7801, 702, 264, 11174, 12987, 4426, 20311, 13, 1752, 1817, 17122, 315, 11174, 11, 566, 51207, 220, 16, 20, 50122, 13, 5005, 11, 389, 7014, 323, 7589, 11]}
