28 + 12 = 40. Jar C is twice as many as B, so 40 * 2 = 80. Then total is 28 + 40 + 80. \n\n28 + 40 is 68, and 68 + 80 is indeed 148. Hmm, that seems correct. \n\nIs there another way to verify this? Let me check the arithmetic again. 28 + 40 is 68, and 68 + 80 is 148. Yes, that's how it adds up. \n\nWait a second, is there a possibility I added the wrong numbers? Let me re-add them. 28 plus 40. 20 + 40 is 60, 8 + 0 is 8, so total 68. Then 68 plus 80. 60 + 80 is 140, and 8 is 8, so 140 + 8 = 148. Yep, that's correct. \n\nSo, all the steps check out. Therefore, the total number of marbles is 148. \n\nBut let me think again if there's another approach to check. Maybe convert all numbers into equations. Let me try.\n\nLet me use variables. Let me define:\n\nJar A = 28  \nJar B = Jar A + 12 = 28 + 12 = 40  \nJar C = 2 * Jar B = 2 * 40 = 80\n\nTotal marbles = A + B + C = 28 + 40 + 80 = 148. \n\nSame result. So, that seems consistent. \n\nI think that's thorough enough. No steps seem to have any errors. Therefore, I can confidently put the answer as 148 in the boxed notation.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{148}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by analyzing the given information:\n\n- Jar A contains **28 marbles**.\n- Jar B contains **12 more marbles** than Jar A.\n- Jar C contains **twice as many marbles** as Jar B.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Count the marbles in Jar B\n\nSince Jar A has 28 marbles, and Jar B has 12 more, Jar B has:\n\n$$\n28 + 12 = 40 \\text{ marbles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Count the marbles in Jar C\n\nJar C has twice the number of marbles in Jar B, so:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 40 = 80 \\text{ marbles}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Find the total number of marbles in all jars\n\nNow, add the marbles in all three jars:\n\n$$\n28 \\text{ (Jar A)} + 40 \\text{ (Jar B)} + 80 \\text{ (Jar C)} = 148\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{148}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jar A has 28 marbles. Jar B has 12 more marbles than jar A. Jar C has twice as many marbles as jar B. How many marbles are there altogether?", "answer": "Jar B has 28 + 12 = <<28+12=40>>40 marbles.\nJar C has 2 x 40 = <<2*40=80>>80 marbles.\nTherefore, there are 28 + 40 + 80 = <<28+40+80=148>>148 marbles altogether.\n#### 148", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jar A has 28 marbles. Jar B has 12 more marbles than jar A. Jar C has twice as many marbles as jar B. How many marbles are there altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["148"], "solution": "148"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30551, 362, 702, 220, 17, 23, 3594, 38763, 13, 30551, 425, 702, 220, 16, 17, 803, 3594, 38763, 1091, 29595, 362, 13, 30551, 356, 702, 10917, 438, 1657, 3594, 38763, 438, 29595, 425, 13, 2585, 1657, 3594], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 358, 1184, 311, 11625, 419, 3491, 911, 3594, 38763, 304, 2326, 60011, 13, 6771, 752, 1191, 553, 14719, 432, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 4710, 5338, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 30551, 362, 702, 220, 17, 23, 3594, 38763, 13, 5005, 11, 30551, 425, 702, 220, 16, 17, 803, 3594, 38763, 1091, 30551, 362, 13, 8704, 30551, 362, 702, 220]}
