ow, after receiving the 5 cookies from her mother, she has 10 (from the first part) plus 5 (from the mother) which totals 15 cookies? Wait, no. Wait, hold on. Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, initially, Sabrina had 20. She gave 10 to her brother. So she had 10 remaining. Then her mother gave her half of what she gave her brother. Half of 10 is 5. So now, Sabrina has 10 + 5 = 15 cookies? Or did she receive the 5? Wait, the original problem says \"Her mother gave Sabrina half the number of cookies she gave her brother.\"\n\nSo the mother gave her half the number of cookies she gave her brother. Which is 10. So half of 10 is 5. So Sabrina now has her original cookies minus 10 (which was given to her brother) plus the 5 cookies from her mother. Therefore, total is 10 + 5 = 15 cookies? Wait, no, hold on. Because she had already given 10 to her brother. Then, the mother gave her half of 10, which is 5. So the mother gave her her brother 10, so that's subtracted, and then added her 5. So total is 25? No, no. Let me think again.\n\nFirst, initial amount is 20. She gives 10 to her brother. So after that, she has 20 - 10 = 10. Then her mother gives Sabrina half of the number of cookies she gave her brother. So Sabrina gave her brother 10, so half of that is 5. Therefore, she is given 5 more cookies. So now total cookies is 10 + 5 = 15. Wait, but does that mean she now has 15 cookies? Yes, that seems right.\n\nThen Sabrina gives two-thirds of her cookies to her sister. How much is two-thirds of her current 15? Let's calculate that. 15 divided by 3 is 5, so two-thirds would be 5. Therefore, she gives 15*(2/3)=10 cookies to her sister. Wait, 15*(2/3)=10? Yes, because if you divide 15 by 3, you get 5, and multiply by 2 to get 10. So Sabrina is giving her sister 10 cookies. \n\nNow, the question is how many cookies are left. So starting with 20, after all these steps, what is left?\n\nFirst step: 20 - 10 = 10. Then add 5 from mother: 10 + 5 = 15. Then subtract 10 cookies she gav