nk equals 20 tanks. \n\nWait, let me check that again. If each tank is 500 liters, then 500 times 20 is 10,000, which matches exactly. So, yes, he needs 20 tanks. \n\nBut hold on, let me make sure there isn't any trick here. Sometimes problems can have hidden steps or nuances. For example, maybe he needs to consider if the tanks can be combined or something else? But the problem says he needs to buy tanks to fill all the balloons, so I think it's straightforward. \n\nAlternatively, let's think about it another way. The total required is 10,000 liters. Each tank is 500 liters, so 500 liters per 10,000 liters... Wait, 10,000 divided by 500 is 20, so 20 tanks. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 20, which means John needs to buy 20 tanks of gas.\n\nLet me just double-check the arithmetic. 500 liters per tank. 500 multiplied by 20 is indeed 10,000, so that matches. If there was any mistake in the problem, the logic would be the same. For example, maybe the question was about if it's better to buy more tanks, but it's not; the question is how many tanks he needs to buy. \n\nSo, the answer should be 20. Therefore, boxed answer is \\boxed{20}.\n</think>\n\nJohn needs to purchase the number of 500-liter gas tanks required to fill the balloons. \n\nEach balloon holds 10 liters of air, and there are 1000 balloons, so the total volume of air required is:\n\n$$\n1000 \\times 10 = 10,000 \\text{ liters}\n$$\n\nHe needs exactly 10,000 liters of gas. Since each 500-liter tank holds 500 liters, and the total required is 10,000 liters, the number of tanks required is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10,000}{500} = 20\n$$\n\nThus, the number of gas tanks John needs to buy is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{20}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?", "answer": "He needs 1000*10=<<1000*10=10000>>10000 liters of gas\nSo he needs 10000/500=<<10000/500=20>>20 tanks\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: John buys 1000 balloons.  Each balloon holds 10 liters of air.  If he buys 500-liter tanks of gas, how many tanks does he need to buy to fill all the balloons?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3757, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 220, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 220, 1416, 566, 49531, 220, 20, 15, 15, 85594, 25866, 315, 6819, 11, 1246, 1657, 25866, 1558], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 3757, 6801, 311, 3695, 69480, 323, 25866, 311, 5155, 1105, 705, 13, 6771, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 419, 700, 3019, 553, 3019, 13, 5512, 11, 1077, 752, 1349, 279, 3491, 1549, 311, 1281, 2704, 358, 3535, 432, 12440, 382, 13079, 49531, 220, 16, 15, 15, 15, 69480, 13, 8886, 46756, 9982, 220, 16, 15, 92866, 315, 3720, 13, 1416]}
