r year, it's 400 * 52. Let me compute that. Let's break down 400 * 52. Since 400 * 52 can be 400*(50 + 2) = 400*50 + 400*2 = 20,000 + 800 = 20,800.\n\nAlternatively, thinking of 52 weeks, which is roughly 3.9 times a year. Since 400 * 52 is 20,800. So the answer should be $\\boxed{20800}$ dollars in the new job compared to the old job.\n\nLet me double-check the calculations to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. New weekly earnings: 20 * 40 = 800. Old weekly earnings: 16 * 25 = 400. Weekly difference: 400. 400 * 52 weeks = 20,800. Yes, that seems right. So the answer is $\\boxed{20800}$.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{20800}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?", "answer": "He makes 20*40=$<<20*40=800>>800 per week\nHe used to make 16*25=$<<16*25=400>>400 per week\nSo his raise was 800-400=$<<800-400=400>>400 per week\nSo he makes 400*52=$<<400*52=20800>>20,800 per year more\n#### 20800", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Jame gets a raise to $20 per hour and works 40 hours a week.  His old job was $16 an hour for 25 hours per week.  How much more money does he make per year in his new job than the old job if he works 52 weeks a year?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20800"], "solution": "20800"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 92088, 5221, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 817, 6460, 323, 4278, 220, 19, 15, 4115, 264, 2003, 13, 220, 5301, 2310, 2618, 572, 400, 16, 21, 458, 6460, 369, 220, 17, 20, 4115, 817, 2003, 13], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 2055, 92088, 702, 264, 4828, 311, 400, 17, 15, 81