ould be 20 unused streetlights.\n\nWait, let me check again. 15 squares * 12 streetlights each = 180. 200 - 180 = 20. Yep, that seems right. So the answer should be 20, which is \\boxed{20}.\n</think>\n\nThe New York City Council bought 200 streetlights. There are 15 squares, each requiring 12 streetlights. Calculating the total used streetlights: $ 15 \\times 12 = 180 $. Subtracting this from the total gives $ 200 - 180 = 20 $. \n\n\\boxed{20}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?", "answer": "So the total number of streetlights that will be used is 15 squares * 12 streetlights/square = <<15*12=180>>180 streetlights\nThere will be 200 streetlights - 180 streetlights = <<200-180=20>>20 streetlights unused.\n#### 20", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: The New York City Council bought 200 streetlights to modernize its squares. But they don't have enough squares to use all the streetlights bought. Knowing that there are 15 squares in New York and that each park will have 12 new streetlights bought by the city council, how many unused streetlights will remain?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["20"], "solution": "20"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 576, 1532, 4261, 4311, 9074, 10788, 220, 17, 15, 15, 8592, 13826, 311, 6481, 551, 1181, 31340, 13, 1988, 807, 1513, 944, 614, 3322, 31340, 311, 990, 678, 279, 8592, 13826, 10788, 13, 57633, 429, 1052, 525], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 419, 700, 